full_judgment:
Sheikh Hassan Arif J: Since the questions of law and facts involved in the aforesaid two writ petitions are almost same and similar, they have been taken up together for hearing, and are now being disposed of by this single judgment.

2. Rule in Writ Petition No. 7891 of 2012 was issued in the following terms:

"Let a Rule Nisi be issued calling upon the respondents Nos. 1 & 2 to show cause as to why they should not be directed to formulate a policy/directives for the English Medium Schools in Bangladesh and in particular prevent those schools from realizing from the students any amount as yearly fees, annual charges or re-admission fees and as to why the respondent No. 3 should not be directed to abolish HS Code S069 in view of Article 15 of the Constitution of the People's Republic of Bangladesh and/or pass such other or further order or orders as to this Court may seem fit and proper."

3. Rule in Writ Petition No. 3677 of 2014 was issued in the following terms:

"Let a Rule Nisi be issued calling upon the respondents to show cause as to why they shall not be directed to take effective measures to frame Education Rules regarding Monthly Tuition Fees, Re-admission Fees/Sessions Charge from Play Group to A Level in the English Medium Schools in Bangladesh and further to show cause as to why they shall not be directed to take necessary steps to form a monitoring cell regarding compliance of the order of this Court regarding students' monthly tuition fees, re-admission fees/sessions charge as well as admission fees from Play Group to "A" Level in the English Medium Schools in Bangladesh, and/or pass such other or further order or orders as to this Court may seem fit and proper."

4. Basically, the aforesaid Rules were issued seeking framing of policy or rules, or directives by the Government for regulating the private English Medium Schools in Bangladesh in particular touching upon the realization of tuition fees, admission fees as well as abrupt increase of tuition fees and admission fees of the students by those schools without any specific guidelines or monitoring by any authority of the State.

5. The petitioner in Writ Petition No. 7891 of 2012 is an advocate of this Court who has filed this application on behalf of the guardians of the students of English Medium Schools in Bangladesh as a pubic interest litigation on the ground that the guardians are not in a position to file this writ petition as they might face repercussions from the school authority or their children might face adverse behavior from the school management. The petitioner in Writ Petition No. 3677 of 2014 is the father of two children who study in two private English Medium Schools in Dhaka and as such he has come up with this writ petition as a directly aggrieved person.

Background Facts:

6. The common background facts in both the writ petitions are that, unlike schools under Primary and Secondary School Education Boards in Bangladesh, the English Medium Schools, which have become now very popular among the educated middle class of Divisional Head Quarters in Bangladesh, do not run their activities under any monitoring system either of the government or of any other body. It is stated by the petitioners that, the children of those schools will play vital role in future in the nation building and as such their education system cannot be left to some business enterprises who are only concerned with the business profits through such education and they have professionalized the education system in this country like an ordinary commodity. It is stated that, there are four education systems in Bangladesh, namely 1) Secular education system carried over from colonial past which is basically Bengali medium education system having English as one of the subjects, 2) Religious Education system run by some Madrashas under the Bangladesh Madrasha Education Board where religious education play a dominant role, 3) Vocational education system run under the Bangladesh Technical Education Board (BTEB) and 4) Private English Medium Schools run by some private enterprises mainly for their business purposes and out of these four systems, first three systems are regulated by the government through different enactments, rules and regulations made-thereunder. However, the fourth system, namely the English Medium Schools, is not at all monitored or controlled either by the Education Ministry or any other authority. It is further stated that, though these English Medium Schools are in fact within the ambit of the control of the government through the Education Directorate or Ministry of Education in view of the provisions under the Registration of Private Schools Ordinance, 1962,  বেসরকারী (ইংরেজী মাধ্যম) বিদ্যালয় নিবন্ধন বিধিমালা, ২০০৭ (hereinafter referred to as "2007 Rules") and বেসরকারী প্রাথমিক (বাংলা ও ইংরেজী মাধ্যম) বিদ্যালয় নিবন্ধন বিধিমালা, ২০১১ (hereinafter called "2011 Rules").

7. It is further stated in Writ Petition No. 3677 of 2014 more specifically that, though the provisions under the Rules 7, 9 and 17 of the said 2007 Rules have made specific mandatory provisions for the said schools to have managing committee, represented by teachers, promoters and representatives of the guardians, conferring upon the same the authority to determine the tuition fees of the students, recruitment process of the teachers, educational qualifications of the teachers and accounts of such schools through audited system, the said mandatory provisions are openly flouted by those schools most of which are run by those promoter businessmen/ women, the prime interest of whom is to generate profit out of such education system. Further anxiety of the petitioners, as reflected in the petitions, are that, these schools have been charging unreasonable tuition fees as well as admission fees from the guardians of the students and they do not have any transparent system as to how those tuition fees and admission fees are charged. Not only that, according to the petitioners, these schools take different types of donations from the students in different names, namely re-admission fees or annual fees, academic fees etc when a child is promoted from one class to another class and that some schools have recently introduced a dubious system to realize such fees/donations along with the monthly tuition fees of the students by increasing the monthly tuition fees and thereby have been putting unreasonable pressure on the guardians who have practically become unable to carry the burden of such fees and charges. That while the guardians of the students pay for each and everything in the schools including the costs of toilet tissues to be used by their children, yet the school authorities demand charges in every year under the garb of different names and the guardians of the schools are not able to raise voice against such exorbitant charges fearing repercussions by the teachers or management on their children. It is stated that, though there is specific mandatory provisions in the aforesaid Rules made under the Ordinance of 1962 to keep the accounts of the schools transparent through auditing by professional auditors under the supervisions of the managing committee, most schools do not have any such practice in place and profits generated by those schools are known to nobody including the officials of the concerned Ministry as the provisions under the said Ordinance and Rules are not at all implemented in practice.

8. Under such disorderly situation in the English Medium Schools in Bangladesh, the petitioners have come up before this Court with the aforesaid writ petitions and obtained the aforesaid Rules. Initially, this Court, vide ad-interim order dated 20-6-2012 in Writ Petition No. 7891 of 2012, directed the Education Ministry and DG of Education Directorate to take necessary steps and further directed the English Medium School authorities not to realize any fees like annual charges, re-admission fees, early fees, etc and not to increase tuition fees of the students more than 10% for 2012-2013 sessions and subsequent years. In Writ Petition No. 3677 of 2014 as well, this Court, vide ad-interim order dated 23-4-2014, restrained the English Medium Schools from realizing re-admission fees, sessions charges etc. from the guardians of Play Group to A levels students for a period of 3 (three) months. However, learned advocates now submit that, the said ad-interim orders of the High Court Division have been stayed by the Appellate Division pursuant to Civil Petitions for Leave to Appeal preferred by some English Medium Schools.

9. Rules are opposed by the government (respondent Nos. 1, 5 and 6 in Writ Petition No. 3677 of 2014) as well as by different English Medium Schools (respondent Nos. 8, 18, 20, 21, 22, 25, 29 and 30 in Writ Petition No. 3677 of 2014 and respondent Nos. 4 and 5 in Writ Petition No. 7891 of 2012). The basic case of the government is that, they have formulated proposed Rules for regulating the English Medium Schools thereby fixing the ceiling of tuition fees as well as admission fees of those schools and final promulgation of the said Rules is in the process. The Rules are seriously opposed by the aforesaid English Medium Schools by filing separate affidavits-in-opposition. The basic anxiety of the respondent-schools are that, any regulation, control and direct monitoring of the management and functioning of the said schools will jeopardize the entire private English Medium education system in this country inasmuch as that the entrepreneurs or promoters who have set up those Schools in Bangladesh for imparting education through English Medium under some international education Boards will be discouraged to run such education system and that will ultimately jeopardize the interest of the students and guardians of those schools. Further contention of these respondents are that, since the said schools have been set up by the said entrepreneurs for generating profit like business concerns, these schools should not be compared with any other government-run schools under different education boards and that since sending of children by the guardians in those schools are optional and they themselves have opted to send their children to these expensive schools for getting better education through better facilities, any control or direction on the government to control such English Medium Schools will act as boomerang and thereby will destroy the very English Medium education system in this country which is generating high quality citizens for Bangladesh who are now contributing in the Nation Building as well as making their marks in different international institutions at home and abroad. Further contention of these respondents is that, fixed tuition fees and admission fee structure for all English Medium Schools will be more problematic than it is now inasmuch as that one English Medium School is different from other English Medium School in terms of education, facilities and other logistic supports that they are providing to the students. Therefore, according to them, no uniform tuition fee and admission fee structure can be provided either by order of this Court or by any guidelines or Rules to be framed by the government. This being so, according to them, though a measure for establishing transparency in the education system as well as fee structure and tuition fee structure of these schools may be implemented, no ceiling of such fees or admission fees can be set up practically. As regards direction sought by the petitioners on the government for framing of rules or law in order to regulate these English Medium Schools, it is contended by these respondents that, no such direction on the government legally can be made for making such Rules or law as it is the specific domain either of the parliament or of the government to enact law or to frame Rules or guidelines under the law.

Submissions:

10. As against above backdrops, Mr Badruddoza, Mr Aneek R. Haque, Mr Md JR Khan (Robin), Mr Muntasir Uddin Ahmed, Mr Sheikh Md Zamir Hossain and Ms. Shamim Ara, learned advocates for the petitioners, have basically made the following submissions:

1) That the English Medium Schools and the students therein comprise a big section of the society either by way of the representation of those children or of their guardians and as such those schools cannot be left unregulated in a way as it is now;

2) When there are specific provisions under the aforesaid Ordinance of 1962 as well as the Rules made thereunder for constitution of Managing Committee to be represented by the guardians, teachers and promoters of those schools, Government should take appropriate actions against the English Medium Schools which have not yet formed such managing committee to run the affairs of the schools;

3) That the petitioners have come up before this Court not for reduction of tuition fees' or admission fees. The main concern of the petitioners in these writ petitions is that there is no transparency in fixing the tuition fees and determining the admission fees of the said schools in particular when there are specific provisions in the said Ordinance and Rules made thereunder for maintaining such transparency. In this regard, Mr Aneek R. Haque, learned advocate appearing for the petitioner in Writ Petition 3677 of 2014, has drawn this Court's Attention to various provisions of the said Ordinance of 1962 and Rules of 2007 and 2011, in particular the provisions under Rules 7, 9, 12, 17 and 18 of the Rules of 2007 and similar provisions in the 2011 Rules;

4) By referring to those provisions, Mr Haque submits that, when there are specific provisions in the said Rules for setting up Managing Committee with the representatives of the guardians and teachers, for recruiting teachers by such Managing Committee upon determining their educational and other qualifications; for determining the tuition fees etc of the students in a transparent way and to audit the accounts of the schools by professional auditors, most of the English Medium schools in Bangladesh do not follow those provisions. Not only that, according to him; for violation of those provisions, they do not' incur any sort of strictures or punishments' from the Government or the registering authority in particular when Section 9 of the said Ordinance of 1962 provides for imprisonment in case of violation of those provisions. Mr Haque then submits. that, though those private English Medium Schools have been flouting the provisions of the said Ordinance and Rules, made thereunder on regular basis, not a single instance can be given, by the government side or the registering authority that any action has ever been taken against those schools;

5) Mr Aneek R. Haque, learned advocate, further drawing this Court's attention to Annexure-X9 to the affidavit-in-opposition filed by respondent No. 21 and X2 to the said affidavit, submits that, it is even apparent' from this affidavit that a teacher, who obtained Bachelor of Law from South Africa, has been teaching English literature in that particular school. According to him, this is a common scenario in almost all English Medium Schools where no such standards of recruitment of teachers are maintained and the teachers having degree in Science are commonly teaching either literature or some other subjects for which they do not have any special educational background;

6) Learned advocates appearing for the petitioners, however, submit that though any ceiling of tuition fees for those English Medium Schools is not practically possible, fixation of tuition fees as well as admission fees should be made transparent by appropriate order from this court as the provisions in the said Ordinance and Rules for making the said system transparent have not been followed by the authorities so far.

7) Mr Bodruddoza, learned advocate appearing for the petitioners, additionally submits that, because of different classes at junior levels in those English Medium Schools, namely Play Group, Nursery, KG-1 and KG-2 etc., the children of those schools who take admission in Play Group always lag-behind the students under different education Boards under National Curriculum inasmuch as that the students of English Medium Schools loose at least two extra years for completing those PG, Nursery and KG classes. According to him, this system not only deprives them of their legitimate expectation in future to have better job in terms of their age, they also lag behind the students of National Curriculum in terms of age which haunt them in their entire life. Therefore, according to him, this system of having PG Class, Nursery, KG-1 and KG-2 etc should be abolished in English Medium Schools as, according to him, these extra classes have been set up by the English Medium Schools only to generate more income from the guardians of the students and that they keep those students in those two or three years unnecessarily and take high amount of tuition fees from the guardians at the cost of the future of those children. Therefore, according to him, this system of having these two or three extra classes at the junior levels in those English Medium Schools should be directed to be abolished by this Court so that the students of English Medium Schools may remain at par, in terms of their age with the students of the National Curriculum in Bangladesh. Learned advocate further submits that, when the toddlers at the age of three or four years in our country should spend their time with their parents at home, they are compelled to go to schools regularly which causes mental trauma to them;

8) Learned advocates for the petitioners further submit that, most of the English Medium Schools totally ignore the culture, history etc. of our country in particular the Bengali language and the special historical background behind it. According to them, though some eye-wash cultural functions are organized by those schools on some special days, in practice the children are openly discouraged to speak Bengali and some schools even issue notices compelling the students not to speak Bengali in school compound. Not only that, according to them, because of such alien atmosphere in those schools, the children in those schools even forget how to speak Bengali in a proper way and most of them speak Bengali in a distorted way which is a clear insult to the martyrs of our Bengali language movement. Therefore, according to them, though the parents send their children to those schools for having better English education, it cannot be the expectation of the parents to make those children English-children in particular when this country is unique in terms of its language movement, liberation war and cultural heritage;

11. As against above submissions, Mr Ahsanul Karim, Mr Omar Sadat, Mr Md Asaduzzaman, Ms. Anjuman Ara and Mr Abdur Razzaque, learned advocates representing different respondent-schools, have made the following submissions:

1) Though some of the concerns of the petitioners are genuine and the respondents frankly concede those concerns, under no circumstances a ceiling for tuition fees or admission fees can be set up for the English Medium Schools inasmuch as that the logistic supports and other material supports that are provided by those schools are not equal;

2) Referring to different logistic supports and fee structures of different schools in Dhaka, learned advocates submit that, it cannot be expected that the tuition fee or admission fee of Scholastica School will be same as that of an unknown English Medium School in Dhaka in particular when the schools like Scholastica, Sunbeams, Sunny date etc. provide much more better facilities as well as logistic supports to their students in addition to better educational qualities of the teachers who impart education in those schools;

3) Any direct control or direct regulation of the management and functioning of those schools by the registering authority or any other authority will discourage the entrepreneurs who have set up those schools basically for business purposes. According to them, though those schools have been set up for business purposes, they have now become inseparable part of our education system as the students of English Medium Schools are making huge contribution in the nation building as well as they are contributing in various international institutions as well as business concerns representing the country's image in global platform;

4) Since the English Medium Schools are private concerns, nobody is compelled to send their children to those schools. Only those who can afford to send their children to those schools may do so. Therefore, according to them, even if the fee structures are high in some schools, the parents are at liberty to take their children to other low-fee schools and as such, in an open market economy like Bangladesh, no regulation should be framed for regulating such private concerns who are imparting high quality English Medium education in this country;

5) To maintain the standards of the syllabus of international education boards, it is incumbent on the part of those schools to maintain a minimum standard of education as well as logistic facilities for which it is reasonable that the tuition fees and admission fees of those schools will be much higher than the schools regulated by the government under National Curriculum system. Therefore, since the children of the petitioners as well as other children are at liberty to move from English Medium Schools to National Curriculum Schools, the management of English Medium Schools should not be compelled to do or not to do anything as regards fees etc. In this regard, Mr Omar Sadat, learned advocate appearing for the respondent No. 18, has referred to various decisions of this subcontinent in particular the case of TMA Pai Foundation vs State of Karnataka, (202) SCC (Volume 8) 481 wherein it was held that the private colleges have the right to admit students of their choices and to set up a reasonable fee structure for those students. It was further held in that case that, bureaucratic or governmental interference in the administration of such institution will undermine its independence in running the affairs of those institutions;

6) Further referring to Cochin University of Science and Technology vs Thomas P. John, (2008) SCC (Volume 8) 82, wherein it was held that "fixation of a fee is a 'part of the administration of an educational institution and it would impose a heavy onus on such an institution to be called upon to justify the levy of a fee with mathematical precision", learned advocate submits that, no such direction should be issued by this Court for fixation of fees with mathematical precision which is practically impossible.

7) Mr Ahsanul Karim, Learned advocate appearing for respondent Nos. 20, 22, 25 and 29 in Writ Petition No. 3677 of 2014 and respondent No. 4 and 5 in Writ Petition No. 7891 of 2012, further submits that, under the Constitutional scheme of this country, no direction can be given by this Court either on the parliament or on the government to enact law or frame rules or regulations on a particular issue as it is the exclusive domain of the parliament and the government to do so as part of their law and policy making decision. In this regard, Mr Karim has referred to the comments of late Mr Mahmudul Islam in his book, Constitution Law of Bangladesh (Third Edition), Paragraph No. 5.89 at page 750 and Bangladesh vs Shafiuddin Ahmed, 50 DLR (AD) 26 and State of Jammu and Kashmir vs AR Zakki, AIR 1992 (SC) 1546;

8) Learned advocates appearing for the respondents, in the course of arguments, however, have frankly conceded that there should be transparency in the running of the affairs of the English Medium Schools in Bangladesh. However, according to them, direct administrative control by the government authorities on those schools will jeopardize the independence of the management of those schools in running the affairs of the same. According to them, some of the English Medium Schools have been performing excellently in particular when the issues of education and quality teachers are involved.

Deliberations of the court:

12. It appears from materials on record, in particular from the provisions of the Registration of Private Schools Ordinance, 1962 ("the said Ordinance") that, the said Ordinance was promulgated with the following preamble:

"Whereas in order to supervise and regulate the working of private schools in Bangladesh, it is expedient to provide for registration of such schools".

13. Therefore, though the said Ordinance was promulgated for the purpose of registration of private schools in Bangladesh, the main purpose of such registration was to supervise and regulate the working of private schools in Bangladesh. The said Ordinance has defined the Higher Secondary Education, Junior Secondary School Education, Nursery Education, Primary Education etc. and the definition of private school under the said Ordinance, as provided by Section 2(g) of the same, is as follows:

"2(g) "private school" means any institution including "O" and "A" level, nursery, Junior Cambridge, Senior Cambridge, International Baccalaureate or equivalent institution established and run by a person or body of persons, not being the Government or a local council for the purpose of imparting organised instruction to ten or more children at a time, but shall not include an institution which is recognised by the government or a Board of Intermediate and Secondary Education or other prescribed authority;"

<u>14. Thus, according to the above definition, the respondent English Medium Schools and all private English Medium Schools come under the definition of private school. Therefore, they are to be remained under the supervision of Registering Authority as defined by Clause (h) of Section 2 of the said Ordinance which is either Board of Intermediate and Secondary Education or other prescribed authority or Directorate of Primary Education or Directorate of Secondary and Higher Secondary Education.</u>

15. Section 3 of the said Ordinance has made it mandatory for all private schools including the respondent schools to have their schools registered under the said Ordinance, and, in obtaining such registration, they are to comply with certain requirements of prescribed standards, qualities, prescribed books, study materials as formulated by the Registering Authority. While Section 8 of the said Ordinance has empowered the government to frame Rules to give effect to the provisions of the said Ordinance by Gazette Notification, Section 9 has provided that any violation by any schools of those Rules and the provisions of the said Ordinance will draw a mischief punishable with imprisonment for a term which may extend to one year or a fine which may extend to Taka 10,000 or with both.

16. Therefore, it appears that, the said Ordinance has made provisions for regulating the private schools in this Country. Therefore, under no circumstances, it can be argued that, private English Medium Schools, being private business concerns, cannot be regulated by any authority. When the said private concerns or individuals set up those private schools, they obtained' registration from the Registering Authority. Thus, they must be presumed to be aware of this law which was promulgated even before emergence of Bangladesh.

17. In addition to the provisions of the said Ordinance, the government has, in 2007, formulated specific Rules in exercise of power under Section 8 of the said Ordinance under the title বেসরকারী (ইংরেজী মাধ্যম) বিদ্যালয় নিবন্ধন বিধিমালা, ২০০৭ ("Rules of 2007") covering all the affairs of the private English Medium Schools in Bangladesh either primary, Secondary or higher Secondary. Elaborate provisions have been made in the said Rules of 2007 for registration as well as running of the affairs of the said schools. Rule 7 of the said Rules of 2007 has made provisions for mandatorily having Managing Committee for each school represented by one guardian, one teacher and 8 promoters. Not only that, the functions of such Managing Committee have been elaborately mentioned under Rule 9, which includes appointment of teachers, employees, determination of fees of the students, auditing of accounts of the schools, maintenance of educational standard, environment etc.

<u>18. Therefore, it appears that, the private English Medium Schools must be run by the Managing Committees to be set up in accordance with Rule 7 of the said Rules of 2007. This means, under no circumstances, a private English Medium School, can be run solely by the promoters or the enterprises or companies which established those schools. There may be prescribed regulations or guidelines for election of guardian member or teacher member in the said Managing Committee, but under no circumstances, setting up of Managing Committee can be ignored on the ground that such election of guardian member and teacher member might cause chaotic situation in English. Schools like some private Bengali medium schools and that the guardians might get involved in politics. When the law has specifically provided for setting up of Managing Committees, so long as that law exists, nobody can raise any other issues for flouting the mandatory provisions of that law. Additionally, when the said Managing Committee has been conferred with the responsibility of recruiting teachers, employees and determining the fee structure of the students as well as auditing of accounts, no other authority can take over that power of the Managing Committee. Any usurpation of that power of the Managing Committee, either by the promoters of those private schools or by individuals behind those private schools, will be a clear violation of the said provisions which will constitute an offence punishable with imprisonment as provided by Section 9 of the said Ordinance. Thus, it is the responsibility of the concerned officials of the Registering Authority to lodge complaint against those members or those enterprises or promoters before a competent Court in view of the provisions under Section 10 of the said Ordinance once such violations are established. Not only that, if the concerned officials of the Registering Authority fail to comply with such responsibilities, appropriate departmental actions should be taken against them for their negligence in duty. This Court is not only concerned with the independence of the management of the said English Medium Schools but is more concerned with the interest of the children in those schools who are taking education. Just because interference from the Registering Authority in accordance with law might jeopardize the independence of some individuals behind those schools, this Court cannot give such enterprises and promoters a blank cheque to run the affairs of those schools in whatever way they like in particular when there are specific provisions for monitoring those schools under the said Ordinance of 1962 and the Rules of 2007.</u>

<u>19. When this Court acknowledges the concerns of the learned advocates for the respondent-schools that the respondent schools are like private business concerns and they have profit motive, it cannot be ignored that this business concerns are dealing with the business of education which is not a commodity like any other business commodities in this country. Of course, they are entitled to run their occupation and profession as guaranteed by our Constitution. At the same time, however, they cannot jeopardize the interest of the nation by running the affairs of those schools in whatever way they like, in particular when the future of the nation heavily relies on the future of the students of those schools. Therefore, when Rule 17 of the said Rules of 2007 has provided for fixation of tuition fees by the Managing Committee, such tuition fees cannot be fixed up by those enterprises and individuals behind those enterprises unilaterally. It is true that there cannot be any uniform or fixed ceiling as regards the tuition or admission tees in particular when the standards of education and logistic supports that the English Medium Schools provide are not equal. Some schools provide more logistic supports and material supports than other schools. When a particular school building is rented in Dhanmondi or Gulshan Area, it is expected that the students of those schools will pay a little bit higher fees than the students of schools which are situated at Wari or old Dhaka area. But that does not allow the concerned authorities of those schools to bypass the approval of such tuition fees or admission fees by the Managing Committee of those schools as constituted in accordance with law. Therefore, when sub-rule (2) of Rule 17 of the said Rules of 2007 has provided not to realize any fees in the name of ভর্তি নবায়ন or পূণঃ ভর্তি, no school can charge any such fees when a student is promoted from one class to another class. While admission fees may be taken once from a student when a particular student is taking admission for the first time, no fees can be charged from him or his guardians when he is promoted from one class to another.</u>

<u>20. Besides, when Rule 18 of the said Rules of 2007 has provided for auditing of the accounts of the schools by the chartered accountant firms, this should be regarded as a mandatory provision to be complied with by each English Medium School. It has to be borne in mind that, the language of the said sub-rule (2) of Rule 18 is very much clear which provides for maintenance of transparency, discipline and accountability as regards accounts of a particular school (আর্থিক শৃঙ্খলা, স্বচ্ছতা ও জবাবদিহীতা নিশ্চিত করণের লক্ষ). Therefore, for maintaining discipline, transparency and accountability of the schools as regards accounts and financial transactions of the same, it is incumbent on the Managing Committee of the said schools to get the accounts of the schools audited by a professional chartered accountant every year and to submit such audited report to the Registering Authority. Since the Rules of 2011, namely বেসরকারী প্রাথমিক (বাংলা ও ইংরেজী মাধ্যম) বিদ্যালয় নিবন্ধন বিধিমালা, ২০১১, 2011, are meant only for the primary private education, this Court is of the view that, since most of the English Medium Schools in this country consist of primary, secondary and higher secondary education, the provisions in the said Rules of 2007 will be applicable in respect of the respondent-schools in this writ petition as well as other private English Medium Schools in this country.</u>

21. It further appears from the materials on record that, probably pursuant to several writ petitions filed by different parties, the government has taken an initiative to formulate a new নীতিমালা (Regulations) under the name বেসরকারী ইংরেজী মাধ্যম স্কুল এবং কলেজ শিক্ষার্থী ভর্তি নীতিমালা ২০১৩. This Court is of the view that, when there are specific provisions in the Rules of 2007 promulgated under the said Ordinance of 1962, this proposed Nitimala, if implemented, will not serve any practical purpose. Rather, it might create more confusion among the guardian and school management in particular when the said proposed guidelines under Rule 9 has put up a ceiling of admission fees and tuition fees etc. This Court is of the view that, this area of fixing up of tuition fees and admission fees should be left to the Managing Committee of each school as constituted in accordance with the provisions of 2007 Rules, and unless and until such Managing Committee is constituted in any particular school, the tuition fees and admission fees of that school has to be priorly approved by the Registering Authority. As stated above, while it is not practical to fix up the ceiling for tuition fees and admission fees given the different standard of educational system as well as logistical supports provided by different schools involving different levels of costs, the fixation of admission fees and tuition fees should be made transparent at the highest level and should be decided by the Managing Committee as constituted in accordance with the said provisions in a transparent manner having proper representation from the guardians of the schools. In this regard, the Government should think of amending the relevant provisions of the said Rules of 2007 in order to increase the number of guardian representatives in the Managing Committee so that the promoters do not get any unreasonable upper-hand in running the affairs of the school. It is also the obligation of the Registering Authority under the said Ordinance as well as Rules framed thereunder to monitor the implementation of the said provisions in particular when the issue of learning Bengali language, Bengali culture and history of Bangladesh are concerned.

22. With the above observations on the prevailing law and prevailing situation of those English Medium Schools, this Court is inclined to give the following directions on the Registering Authority as well as on all the English Medium Schools in Bangladesh.

Directions:

1) There must be a Managing Committee to be constituted in strict compliance with the provisions under Rule 7 of the বেসরকারী (ইংরেজী মাধ্যম)বিদ্যালয় নিবন্ধন বিধিমালা, ২০০৭ (Rule of 2007) in each and every English Medium School in Bangladesh within a period of 03 (three) months from the receipt of the copy of this judgment; The Government should take steps to amend the relevant provisions of the said Rules 63 increase the number of guardian representatives in the Managing Committee in line with our above observations;

2) The Managing Committee of each school shall hold their meeting at least once in two months and discuss and determine all issues as regards affairs and management of the concerned school independently;

3) It is the Managing Committee only which will recruit the teachers and employees of each and every English Medium School, and in such recruiting process, neither the promoters nor the individuals behind the private enterprises which set up those schools will have any role to play;

4) Any dissenting view in the meeting of the Managing Committee by the guardian member of the Managing Committee has to be taken into consideration with outmost importance and that dissenting view cannot be overruled out rightly without assigning specific and reasonable reasons given that, according to the existing Rules, Managing Committee will have majority number promoters;

5) It is, the Managing Committee which will fix up the tuition fees, admission fees and other charge to be imposed on the students and their guardians. No fees in the name of session fee, re-admission fee, renewal fee, academic fee etc. can be charged on the students who are promoted from one class to another. Such fees cannot also be realized indirectly by increasing monthly tuition fees in order to frustrate the order of this Court; The schools which in the meantime have increased their tuition fees to realize such session fees etc. are directed to immediately go back to the earlier position in respect of tuition fees;

6) Apart from tuition lees and admission fees, if the Managing Committee decides to impose any other charges on the students and guardians, the representation of the guardians must be taken into  consideration on that particular charge prior to such imposition and, in this regard; the dissenting view, if any of the guardian member in the Managing. Committee has to be taken into consideration with outmost importance and can only be overruled by assigning specific reasonable reasons;

7) While the Managing Committee is empowered to impose other charges on the students particularly when the logistic supports as well as cost of running the affairs of the school are increased, such charges can only be imposed after considering the proper representation of the guardians in. a very transparent manner, if necessary, after holding prior meetings with the guardians of the schools. Abrupt imposition of charges or increase of tuition fees is highly deprecated by this Court.

8) It has to be borne in mind that the guardians of the private English Medium Schools have chosen this system for their children's better education knowing fully well that those English Medium Schools will be more expensive than the schools under the national curriculum system. Therefore, that expectation cannot be compromised just because the fees of some schools are a little bit higher;

9) It is the Managing Committee which is solely authorized to recruit the teachers and employees of a particular school by giving proper prior notification. No back-door appointment in the name of master roll, pay roll or temporary appointment or trainee teacher appointment is allowed in those schools;

10) It is the Managing Committee which will determine the educational and other qualifications of a teacher to be recruited for that particular school and no compromise should be made in respect of such quality.

11) The Managing Committee shall give utmost importance to the transparency of the accounts of a private English Medium School given that the main allegation of the petitioners in this writ petition is regarding this sector. It is true that, the private enterprises as well as the individuals who set up these private schools have profit motives. But they are not expected to generate unreasonable profits by indulging themselves in a profession of education and thereby making the education as an ordinary commodity like any other commodity in this country. Therefore, the Managing Committee shall have the accounts of the school audited each year by a competent private auditor and such audit report has to be submitted before the Registering Authority. After such audit, the guardians of the school shall be entitled to have copy of the said audit report through the website of that school or through other convenient means;

12) While the Managing Committee of each school and the school authorities are bound to follow the syllabus of different international education boards for Ordinary Levels (O Levels) and Advance Level (A Levels) students, the Managing Committee should determine the syllabus and educational materials for the students up to class VIII (eight). In formulating such syllabus, they must keep in mind the history of our Bengali nation and its cultural heritage as well as the history of independence of this country. It has to be given utmost importance that history of our Bengali language is unique in this world and that should be reflected in the syllabus of the students of these schools up to class VIII (eight).

13) When it is expected that the students of English Medium Schools will speak in English in the campus and will give more importance in English language than Bengali as a medium of their education, that being the purpose of sending them to those schools, but giving more importance to English language should not in any way make them ignorant of the Bengali language. The school Managing Committee shall formulate specific guidelines for imparting Bengali education (reading, writing and speaking) in those schools at least up to class VIII (eight). In formulating that guidelines, it has to be kept in mind that special measures be taken so that the students of those schools do not hesitate to speak, write and read Bengali in any forum and that they do not speak Bengali in any distorted way;

14) While taking decisions about recruiting teachers of those English medium schools, it is incumbent on the Managing Committee to formulate a guideline for such recruitment so that the guardians know in advance as to what types of teachers are to be there for teaching their children and such guidelines should be published through websites of those schools;

15) The Managing Committee must give utmost priority regarding maintenance of standard of quality of teachers, and after recruitment, they will arrange training of those teachers if they do hot have any prior training on teaching the children;

16) The Managing Committee of each school shall positively decide forthwith whether they will keep so many classes like PG, Nursery, Kg-1 and Kg-2 etc. for toddlers even before reaching Class-One or Standard One in those schools in particular when the children of English Medium Schools are admittedly losing one or two years by attending those additional classes. In this regard, they will take into consideration the loss of ages by the children of those schools in comparison to the students of the schools under national curriculum system. Keeping extra classes for junior students for additional income is highly deprecated by this Court.

17) The Managing Committee of the schools shall arrange, in addition to the observation of the national days like Language Day, Independence Day, Victory Day, some other designated days for celebrating the cultural heritage of Bengali nation in particular the heritage that we got trough the literature as provided by Rabindranath Tagore and Kazi Nazrul Islam, and make the students conversant with those heritage. Special measures should be taken to educate the students the history of Bangladesh as well as the history of our language movement and the contribution of the father of the nation Bangabandhu Sheikh Mozibur Rahman and other renowned freedom fighters in the liberation war of Bangladesh.

23. Since we are not giving any sort of direction on the government or any authority to frame any Rules or guidelines, rather we are giving directions on the concerned authorities to implement the present law as it is in the way as observed by us in this judgment, we do not need to discuss the issues whether this Court has any jurisdiction to pass order on the government to frame such Rules.

24. Though the aforesaid directions are given apparently on private English Medium Schools by this Court, they are in fact the directions on the concerned Ministry and Registering Authority for the implementation of the Ordinance of 1962 and Rules of 2007 or any other similar Rules including their amendments. Therefore, it is incumbent upon the Registering Authority as well as the concerned officials of the Education Ministry to make sure that those directions are implemented in its strict sense. It is incumbent on them to initiate proceedings against the delinquent individuals of the schools concerned in view of the provisions under Section 9 of the Registration of Private School Ordinance, 1962 if they find any established violation of the said provisions and the directions of this Court hereinabove.

25. The aforesaid directions will remain as Writ of Continuous Mandamus as issued by this Court on the concerned authorities as well as the respondent schools and other English Medium Schools of this country. It is the responsibility of the Registering Authority to circulate these directions to all English Medium Schools within a period of one month from receipt of the copy of this judgment and orders. The Registering Authority is directed to intimate this Court by way of a report as regards implementation and steps taken for such implementation of the aforesaid directions within six months from receipt of the copy of this judgment.

26. Before end, we thank all the learned advocates who have assisted us with their research and submissions in the course of hearing.

Communicate this.

